# [EODIR](../wg/eodir.html)

## Summary

The Working Group Chairs Forum (EODIR session) at IETF 127 covered legal guidance for WG chairs, proposed moderation procedures and guidelines, IAB and EODIR outreach initiatives, updates to the RFC Editor website (`rfceditor.org`), and a historical archiving project for past WG chairs.

## Key Discussion Points

### IETF Counsel Update
Brad Biddle and Vigdis (IETF Counsel) provided an overview of legal considerations relevant to working group chairs:
* **Antitrust and Commercial Influence:** Chairs were reminded to review the IETF antitrust guidelines. Red flags include detailed discussions of pricing or allowing dominant market players to dictate standards direction. Decisions must remain strictly grounded in technical merit, rough consensus, running code, and considerations for open-source implementers and absent parties.
* **Intellectual Property and Trademarks:** Disclosing or negotiating specific RAND licensing terms should be avoided within working group discussions. Inclusion of third-party trademarks in Internet-Drafts/RFCs should be avoided whenever possible to prevent subjective legal issues surrounding origin and likelihood of confusion.
* **Interactions with Regulations and Law:** Participants discussed how regulators increasingly reference or mandate IETF specifications (e.g., AI preferences, BGP security). Counsel noted that while the IETF develops specifications voluntarily based on technical merit, mandatory regulatory adoption alters implementation dynamics. Daniel Gillmor and Paul Wauters noted the importance of understanding the real-world legal and regulatory contexts in which technical standards are deployed.

### Moderation Procedures Update
Bron Gondwana presented an update on behalf of the IETF Moderator Team regarding updated draft procedures for handling disruptive behavior across IETF forums:
* **Procedures and Ladder:** The proposed moderation procedures establish an incident counter and a maximum remedy ladder (up to a 5-year lockout for severe repeat offenders, with a 3-day default/initial remedy). Incidents expire after 5 years.
* **Definitions of Participants:** Gondwana highlighted a companion draft in the GEN area specifying that IETF participation must be by individual human beings. Sock puppets, unmonitored team addresses, and automated AI agents acting without explicit human accountability are prohibited from consensus building.
* **Chair Discretion and Warnings:** Remedies represent maximum caps, not mandatory minimums; chairs retain full discretion to use minimal interventions, issue informal warnings (which do not count as formal incidents), or rescind incidents filed in error.
* **Existing PR Actions:** Current BCP 83 Peer Review (PR) actions affecting five individuals will expire upon approval of the new procedures, but repeat misbehavior will immediately escalate those individuals to Level 3 (90-day lockout).

### IAB Outreach Update
Yaroslav Rosomakho (IAB Outreach Coordinator) presented on IETF outreach efforts:
* **Scope and Channels:** Outreach efforts focus on encouraging IETF participation, evangelizing open standards, and engaging external technical communities (such as RIPE, NANOG, LACNIC) and policy venues (such as IGF in Nairobi).
* **Resources:** The IAB maintains a slide library and a tracking wiki (`https://wiki.ietf.org/group/eodir/outreach`). Participants presenting on IETF work at external conferences are encouraged to notify `outreach@iab.org` and share presentation materials.
* **Individual vs. Official Status:** Mike Ounsworth and Yaroslav Rosomakho noted that chairs and participants presenting externally should clearly disclaim personal opinions from official IETF or working group consensus.

### RFC Editor Web Interface Update
Alexis Rossi (RFC Series Consulting Editor) demonstrated new end-user features on `rfceditor.org`:
* **Subscriptions and Sets:** Users can create accounts to subscribe to notifications for RFC updates, errata, and obsoletions, as well as create custom sets of RFCs.
* **Accessibility Features:** Added a read-aloud feature, adjustable reading modes (font sizes, word spacing, playback speed), and improved keyboard navigation.
* **Subject Taxonomy:** RFCs are categorized under a subject taxonomy, enabling users to browse and subscribe to specific technical topics.
* **Author Affiliations:** Mark Nottingham pointed out that author affiliations were omitted from the top header on the updated RFC views. Rossi confirmed this was unintentional feedback to be addressed.

### WG Chairs History Program
Robert Sparks reported on an initiative to reconstruct a complete historical record of past working group chairs in the Datatracker:
* Using automated agents to parse historical mailing lists, meeting proceedings, and chat logs to recover missing data prior to the mid-2000s.
* Requested long-time participants active in the 1990s and early 2000s to review extracted records for accuracy.

## Decisions and Action Items

* **Moderation Procedures Feedback:** Working group chairs and community members are asked to review and provide feedback on the proposed moderation procedures on GitHub (`https://github.com/ietf-moderators/procedures`) prior to IESG approval.
* **Outreach Materials:** Chairs and presenters are encouraged to submit slides and notify `outreach@iab.org` regarding external speaking engagements.

## Next Steps

* **Moderation Procedures Approval:** The Moderator Team will finalize the procedure text and submit it to the IESG for formal approval.
* **WG Chair History Verification:** Robert Sparks will deploy a public preview of historical WG chair data for community review and verification.